00:10:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a really sharp observation, and it all comes down to two things: **contrast** and **what we notice.** When you toss a red item in with whites, even the tiniest amount of loose red dye that bleeds into the water will deposit onto those white fibers. White is a blank canvas—literally no background color to hide the dye. So you immediately see a pale pink tint. It’s like putting a single drop of red food coloring into a glass of clear water; you can’t miss it. Now, when you wash a mixed load of colors, the same kind of dye bleeding can absolutely still happen—a new red shirt might release a little dye, a dark blue jeans might shed some indigo, etc. So why don’t we see the colors “mix” into a muddy mess? A few reasons: 1. **Dilution and distribution.** The small amount of dye gets spread across every garment in the load. If a red sock bleeds, that loose dye is absorbed by a dozen different items—each one already has its own color. The little bit of extra dye might shift the hue imperceptibly on each piece, but because the base color is already there, the change isn’t obvious. On a dark navy T-shirt, a hint of extra red won’t turn it purple in any noticeable way. On a light gray shirt, it might leave a faint pinkish cast that you’d chalk up to lighting or never even spot. 2. **Colorfastness varies, but many “colors” loads are chosen wisely.** Clothes sold as brightly colored or “pre-washed” often have better dye fixation. The red item that ruins a white load is usually an outlier—a cheaply dyed garment, not designed to be colorfast. If you had a whole load of equally unstable dyes, they would indeed cross-contaminate, and you’d likely see discoloration (like a white collar or a lighter pastel in the load picking up grey or pink). But this rarely happens because most garments in a “colors” load are already stable, and the loose-dye culprit is diluted and swallowed by the other dark, heavily dyed items whose own colors hide